  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern is currently dominated by a large high pressure \ncentered over Oklahoma and a small trough off the coast of \nCalifornia. The high pressure reaches all the way to the surface, \nbut also has a surface low pressure system along the Kansas/Colorado \nborder. A weak cold front stretches southward from the low. The \nprimary focus of the forecast will be storms associated with this \nsystem along with the lingering chances later into this week. Prior \nto the storms, highs will jump up to 100-110 degrees. As a result, \nan Extreme Heat Warning is in effect for the eastern three counties \nfrom noon until 9 PM. A Heat Advisory is also in effect today for \nthe same time period for the next eastern 6 counties. Apparent \ntemperatures may climb all the way up to 115 towards central Kansas. \nLater in the day, storms are forecast to develop. Even this morning, \na good deal of uncertainty remains. The current thinking is an \naggregate of the prominent CAMS with weak isolated storms initiating \nnear the Colorado border at around 0Z. Storms will then expand in \ncoverage and intensify into the night. The environment is conducive \nfor high-based storms. The shear on forecast soundings is marginal \nat around 30 KTs. As a result, severe hail may struggle to generate \noutside of the strongest storms. The most favorable environment is \nalong a Scott City to Hays line and northward. Otherwise strong \nwinds gusts up to 65 mph is the primary threat. Storms on the \nsouthern edge may see the precipitation fall as virga. If so, dry \nlightning will pose and overnight fire risk and need to be \nmonitored, especially with the frontal passage creating a wind \nshift. Recent trends in CAMs have the convection pushing farther \nnorth so it is quite possible that the majority of the severe threat \nwill occur outside of the CWA. That said, enough CAPE and moisture \nis present for strong storm development; conversely however the lack \nof upper-level dynamical support will greatly limit severity. The \none caveat would be if the currently marginally forecast lapse rates \noverperform, a wind gust up to 75 mph can not be ruled out. \n\nFriday is expected to be a few degrees cooler with the \naforementioned cold front moving through in the morning with most of \nSW Kansas seeing highs in the 90s and lower 100s. Barber county will \nbe by far the warmest with highs again pushing 108 with notable WAA \ninto the afternoon. Later in the day, storms chances very similar to \ntoday is expected with isolated storms trying to make the most of \nmarginal lower-level ingredients. Wind will again be the primary \nthreat with everything else being very marginal if anything at \nall. Storms are again shown by CAMs to initiate around 0Z along \nboth the Colorado/Kansas and Oklahoma/Kansas borders. The NAM \nforecast soundings have even more favorable CAPE and lapse \nrates, but even less shear. This will likely keep the storms too\ndisorganized to take advantage of an environment that would \notherwise be conducive to 70+ mph wind gusts. In the outside \nchance that the potential can be tapped, considerable wind gusts\nare a threat. \n\nDeja vu continues into Saturday looking very similar to Friday \nin temperatures and storms chances. The most prominent \ndifference is that the severe threat is even more marginal with \nthe best chances pushed to the northwest zones. More temporal \nuncertainty is present preventing a plethora of details, but it \nis expected to be a higher- based storm structure with wind \ngusts continuing to be the number one threat. If a lot of rain \nfell in concentrated areas in the previous two days, localized \nflooding concerns may be present. \n\nSunday will be noticeably cooler with highs remaining in the \n80s for much of the area. Ensembles again have a round of storm \nchances Sunday night that appear similar to the past few days. \nThe rest of the week is forecast to be drier with intermittent \nrain chances that remain below 20%. Tuesday's highs will look \nsimilar to Monday before the rest of the work week warms back up\ninto the 90s and even approaching triple digits again. \nThroughout any of the days, especially today, that see very hot \nweather keep precautions in mind by staying hydrated and taking \ncooling breaks.",
